-- title:
--   Non-finite μ_q-type Hopf algebras over ℤ: punctured models
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number, $q$ a prime with $q \neq 2$, and let $K$ be a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $\mathbf{Z}$ which is of finite type as a $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra and flat as a $\mathbf{Z}$-module. Write $\mathbf{Z}_{(\ell)}$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) $\ell$ of $\mathbf{Q}$ consisting of the rationals whose denominator is coprime to $\ell$. Assume: for every prime $\ell \neq p$ the base change $\mathbf{Z}_{(\ell)} \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}} K$ is a finite $\mathbf{Z}_{(\ell)}$-module; the number of $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra homomorphisms $K \to \overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ is exactly $q$; the Galois action on these points is cyclotomic in the sense that for every ring automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ and every $n_\sigma \in \mathbf{N}$ with $\sigma\zeta = \zeta^{n_\sigma}$ for all $\zeta$ with $\zeta^q = 1$, one has $\sigma(\psi k) = \psi^{n_\sigma}(k)$ for every $\psi : K \to_{\mathbf{Z}\text{-alg}} \overline{\mathbf{Q}}$ and $k \in K$, the power being taken in the convolution monoid `WithConv`; and finally that $K$ is *not* a finite $\mathbf{Z}$-module. The conclusion is that $p$ is prime and that there is a $\mathbf{Z}$-bialgebra map $\iota : \mathbf{Z}[\mathrm{Multiplicative}(\mathbf{Z}/q)] \to K$ with the following property for every commutative ring $R$: precomposition with $\iota$ is injective on $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra maps $K \to R$, and a $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra map $g : \mathbf{Z}[\mathrm{Multiplicative}(\mathbf{Z}/q)] \to R$ factors as $g = f \circ \iota$ for some $\mathbf{Z}$-algebra map $f : K \to R$ if and only if there exists $a \in R$ with $a - 1$ in the ideal generated by $p$ such that $a\,(g(h) - \varepsilon(h)) = 0$ for all $h$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit of the group algebra followed by the structure map $\mathbf{Z} \to R$.
--
--   This is the functor-of-points description of the non-finite ("punctured") multiplicative-type group schemes of odd prime order $q$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf{Z}$ occurring in Mazur's analysis of quasi-finite groups: $\operatorname{Spec} K$ is the subgroup functor of $\mu_q$ consisting of the points that are trivial in a neighbourhood of the fibre at $p$, the extension by zero of $\mu_q$ from $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf{Z}[1/p]$, and in particular $p$ must be prime. It is used by the group-scheme statement [`AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_iso_or_natCard_algHom_eq_one_and_exists_shortExact_of_sectionsEquiv_convPow_of_ne_two`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_iso_or_natCard_algHom_eq_one_and_exists_shortExact_of_sectionsEquiv_convPow_of_ne_two), which splits such a group into the trivial case, the honest $\mu_q$ and this punctured case.
